<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 500 BCE · Ancient Near East</p><h1 class="sg-title">Psalm 23</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תהילים כג</bdi> / <bdi lang="hbo-Latn">Tehillim 23</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An ancient Hebrew song that pictures God as a shepherd caring for his sheep, still sung by Jews and Christians today.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditionally ascribed to King David; not known</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>ancient Israelites; Jewish and Christian worshippers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Biblical Hebrew</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>ancient Israel (not known exactly)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditionally ascribed to David; dated by many scholars to after the Babylonian exile</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words anonymous, traditionally ascribed to King David)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a> is the 23rd psalm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Book of Psalms</a>, part of the third section of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and a book of the Christian Old Testament; in the numbering of the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Vulgate it is Psalm 22, known in Latin as &quot;Dominus regit me&quot;. Its heading ascribes it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a>, who in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> Scriptures was a shepherd in his youth. Some scholars doubt this and propose other possibilities, commonly dating the psalm to the post-exilic period. The image of a god or king as shepherd was common in ancient Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> called himself a shepherd who brought well-being to his people in the conclusion to his law code.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> the psalm is traditionally sung at the third Shabbat meal and before the first and second, and in some communities during the Kiddush. It is also recited beside a person who has died, by those keeping watch over the body before burial, and at funerals. Christians connect its shepherd with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, called the Good Shepherd in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> include it in the prayers before receiving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>; the Catholic Church assigns it to the Liturgy of the Hours and sings it as a responsorial at Masses for the dead; the Coptic Agpeya prays it at Terce; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> appoints it for the evening of the fourth day of the month.</p><p>The psalm reached many languages and versions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> is associated with three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> psalters, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myles-coverdale">Myles Coverdale</a>'s prose rendering. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> spread vernacular Bibles, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Bible (1557) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Bible</a> (1611), whose wording became the best known English form of the psalm. Several of its phrases became popular sayings in English.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the psalm was recast as metrical verse to be sung to hymn tunes: The Bay Psalm Book (1640), the Sidney Psalms, and settings by George Herbert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-watts">Isaac Watts</a>. The best known is &quot;The Lord's My Shepherd&quot;, first published in the 1650 Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a>. It is widely attributed to the Parliamentarian Francis Rous, but a translating committee edited it heavily in the 1640s. It is usually sung to the tune Crimond, generally attributed to Jessie Seymour Irvine, and it remains one of the most popular hymns among English-speaking congregations. Henry Baker's &quot;The King of Love My Shepherd Is&quot; (1868) is another hymn drawn from the psalm.</p><p>In the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a> became closely tied to funerals in the English-speaking world, and films often show it recited at a graveside. Official liturgies were slower: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>'s burial service uses Psalms 39 and 90, and the Episcopal Church in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> first used Psalm 23 at funerals in its 1928 prayer book.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A short prayer of trust in which the speaker pictures God as a shepherd who feeds, leads and protects his flock, and as a host who welcomes a guest. Its mood is calm confidence that God stays near even in danger and through the whole of life.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Biblical Hebrew</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button></div><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Biblical Hebrew</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">מִזְמ֥וֹר לְדָוִ֑ד</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">mizmor ledavid</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יְהֹוָ֥ה רֹ֝עִ֗י לֹ֣א אֶחְסָֽר׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">YHWH ro'i lo ekhsar</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">בִּנְא֣וֹת דֶּ֭שֶׁא יַרְבִּיצֵ֑נִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">bin'ot deshe yarbitseni</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">עַל־מֵ֖י מְנֻח֣וֹת יְנַהֲלֵֽנִי׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">'al-mei menukhot yenahaleni</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">נַפְשִׁ֥י יְשׁוֹבֵ֑ב</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">nafshi yeshovev</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">יַֽנְחֵ֥נִי בְמַעְגְּלֵי־צֶ֝֗דֶק לְמַ֣עַן שְׁמֽוֹ׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">yankheni vema'gelei-tsedek lema'an shemo</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">גַּ֤ם כִּֽי־אֵלֵ֨ךְ בְּגֵ֪יא צַלְמָ֡וֶת</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">gam ki-elekh begei tsalmavet</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">לֹא־אִ֘ירָ֤א רָ֗ע</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">lo-ira ra'</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">כִּי־אַתָּ֥ה עִמָּדִ֑י</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">ki-attah 'immadi</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">שִׁבְטְךָ֥ וּ֝מִשְׁעַנְתֶּ֗ךָ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">shivtekha umish'antekha</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">הֵ֣מָּה יְנַֽחֲמֻֽנִי׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">hemmah yenakhamuni</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">תַּעֲרֹ֬ךְ לְפָנַ֨י ׀ שֻׁלְחָ֗ן</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">ta'arokh lefanai shulkhan</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">נֶ֥גֶד צֹרְרָ֑י</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">neged tsorerai</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">דִּשַּׁ֥נְתָּ בַשֶּׁ֥מֶן רֹ֝אשִׁ֗י</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">dishanta vashemen roshi</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">כּוֹסִ֥י רְוָיָֽה׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">kosi revayah</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">אַ֤ךְ ׀ ט֤וֹב וָחֶ֣סֶד יִ֭רְדְּפוּנִי</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">akh tov vakhesed yirdefuni</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">כׇּל־יְמֵ֣י חַיָּ֑י</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">kol-yemei khayyai</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="hbo" dir="rtl">וְשַׁבְתִּ֥י בְּבֵית־יְ֝הֹוָ֗ה לְאֹ֣רֶךְ יָמִֽים׃</span><span class="sg-r" lang="hbo-Latn">veshavti beveit-YHWH le'orekh yamim</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>He maketh me to lie down in green pastures: he leadeth me beside the still waters.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>He restoreth my soul: he leadeth me in the paths of righteousness for his name's sake.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yea, though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil: for thou art with me; thy rod and thy staff they comfort me.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou preparest a table before me in the presence of mine enemies: thou anointest my head with oil; my cup runneth over.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Surely goodness and mercy shall follow me all the days of my life: and I will dwell in the house of the LORD for ever.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Psalm 23, Hebrew Bible (Masoretic text). Text from <a href="https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%AA%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%99%D7%9D_%D7%9B%D7%92" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (SBL general-purpose).</p><p>English: King James Version (1611). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)%2FPsalms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (3)</summary><ul><li>The divine name is romanized as written, YHWH (the pointing is for the reading Adonai, as it is sung).</li><li>Cantillation marks and the paseq (|) are not romanized; qamats katan read as o (kol-yemei).</li><li>Romanization: initial aleph unmarked, other aleph and all ayin as '; dagesh forte doubled except in the digraphs sh, ts, kh; maqqef as a hyphen.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
